From 441e316756fe9fd597a8c20365ec4fe0bc4786b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: L0garithmic <L0garithmic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 13:03:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] weblocks: implement the Web Locks API on Window and
 WorkerNavigator

Servo had no navigator.locks. That is not merely a missing convenience: a page
that calls navigator.locks.request() without a guard gets a TypeError, and when
that happens on the first line of a worker script the worker dies before it can
register its message handler, so it never answers the page and the page waits
forever. web.whatsapp.com does exactly this in its backend worker, which is why
it logs in and then sits on "Loading your chats" forever.

- WebLocks.webidl: LockManager (request with both spec overloads, and query) and
  Lock, behind a new dom_weblocks_enabled pref, exposed on Window and Worker.
- registry.rs: the lock state, process-global and keyed by origin, because a
  window and its workers share an origin and must see the same locks. Waiters
  park a Send waker that queues a task back on their own event loop, so the
  thread that releases a lock can grant it to a waiter on another thread.
  Unit tests cover exclusion, sharing, ifAvailable and steal.
- lockmanager.rs: a granted lock lives exactly as long as the promise its
  callback returned, which is the whole release mechanism; a native handler on
  that promise releases the lock and settles the outer one the same way.

Known deviation: steal drops the previous holder without rejecting its promise
with AbortError, and the AbortSignal option is accepted but not yet honoured.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
